<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phocas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/547">547</a>–<a href="/years/610">610 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phocas was a Byzantine emperor who seized power in 602 CE after a mutiny in which the emperor Maurice was murdered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, who had been restored to power with Maurice's help, used the murder as a pretext for war. The resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-sasanian-war-of-602-628">Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628</a> CE exhausted both empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phocas.jpg" alt="Phocas" width="317" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British Museum · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phocas,_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Phocas was most likely born in 547, since he was said to be 55 when he became emperor, and he was probably of Thraco-Roman or Cappadocian origin. His life before the usurpation is obscure, but he is said to have served as a centurion in the army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> under Emperor Maurice. Late in Maurice's reign the army sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> as spokesman to complain about the conduct of the general Comentiolus; he argued with the emperor and had his beard pulled by an outraged patrician.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In autumn 602 the Thracian army rebelled, largely from exhaustion and outrage at orders to keep campaigning north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in winter, and at earlier wage cuts. The troops made Phocas their leader and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. When riots broke out, Maurice and his family fled on a warship on the night of 22 November 602. Phocas was crowned by the patriarch Cyriacus on 23 November and entered the city on 25 November. Maurice and his sons were soon captured and executed, and Maurice's wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Constantina</a> and their daughters were put to death around 605.</p><p>Phocas distrusted the elite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, to whom he was a usurper and a provincial boor, and filled senior posts with relatives, among them his brother Domentziolus as magister officiorum in 603. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> invaded the eastern provinces, and Phocas, dealing with conspiracies at home, could not stop them. When the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> defected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, Phocas invited him to Constantinople under a promise of safe conduct and had him burnt alive. The Balkan frontier stayed stable, and he made a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> in 604.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 608 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a> appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius-the-elder">Heraclius the Elder</a>, the exarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, to rebel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> cut off the grain supply to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and began his invasion in 609. His son Heraclius reached the capital on 3 October 610 and seized it on 5 October, when he was declared emperor and had Phocas executed. According to the sources, when Heraclius asked, &quot;Is this how you have ruled, wretch?&quot;, Phocas replied, &quot;And will you rule better?&quot;</p><p>The surviving sources are universally hostile to Phocas, describing an incompetent tyrant who purged opposition and left the empire open to invaders. Some of the earliest were written under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, whose dynasty had an interest in blackening his reputation, so how far they can be trusted is hard to judge. Phocas was shown with a beard on coins, which became the convention until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phocas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The immediate trigger was the Byzantine-Sasanian War of 602–628 CE, launched after the emperor Maurice, who had helped restore Khosrow II to power against the usurper Bahram Chobin in 591 CE, was overthrown and murdered by the junior officer <b>Phocas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>According to John the Lydian, the praetorian prefect <b>Phocas</b> funded the first work with 4,000 Roman pounds of gold.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>In 609 the Byzantine emperor <b>Phocas</b> gave it to Pope Boniface IV, who consecrated it on 13 May 609 as a church of St. Mary and the Martyrs; twenty-eight cartloads of martyrs' relics were said to have been brought from the catacombs and placed beneath the high altar,…</blockquote><a href="/work/pantheon">Pantheon · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Theodosius was deposed in a military revolt and was said to have been executed by <b>Phocas</b>'s supporters.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-26">26 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Phocas</b>, leader of the army mutiny that had overthrown Emperor Maurice, had Maurice's five sons executed and then Maurice himself.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-27">27 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The fleet of Heraclius the Younger, who with his father, the Exarch of Africa, had rebelled against Emperor <b>Phocas</b> in 608, reached Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-3">3 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42,26&amp;year=587" aria-label="Thrace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Phocas"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4746 3977h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4746 3977h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42,26&amp;year=587">What was here in 587 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
